---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **작품이 멀리 가면 배우도 더 찾아보나.** (`/actor-reach`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ 날것(3.09배)을 머리에 안 쓴다. 작품 수를 맞추면 흩어지고, 시기를 맞추면 더 흩어진다.
 * ⛔ 얇은 칸은 **배수를 안 낸다.** 열두 명 미만이면 「못 냈다」로 적는다.
 * ⛔ 「인기」라 안 쓴다. 조회는 좋은 일로도 나쁜 일로도 는다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-actor-reach.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-actor-reach.json';

const 하나 = data.bands[0];
const 다섯 = data.bands[3];
const TITLE = 'If your show travelled, do more people look you up?';
const DESC =
  `Joining Netflix's country charts to English Wikipedia page views across ${data.actors} Korean actors, `
  + `those with a title in ${data.wideThreshold}+ countries draw ${data.overall.times}x the median lookups — `
  + `but once title count and recency are held fixed, only actors with a single charting title keep the gap.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
const 배 = (v) => (v == null ? 'too thin to say' : `${v}×`);
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/actor-reach">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This is a join of two tables we keep: which countries a Korean title charted in, and how often
    people opened its actors’ English Wikipedia pages. {data.actors} actors appear in both.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>The raw gap is {data.overall.times}× — and most of it is size</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Charting titles</th><th>Actors</th><th>Best title under {data.wideThreshold} countries</th>
            <th>{data.wideThreshold}+ countries</th><th>Ratio</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="edge">
            <td class="nm">All of them</td>
            <td>{data.actors}</td>
            <td>{data.overall.narrowActors} actors · {data.overall.narrowMedian.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td>{data.overall.wideActors} actors · {data.overall.wideMedian.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{배(data.overall.times)}</td>
          </tr>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td>{b.actors}</td>
              <td>{b.narrowActors} · {b.narrowMedian == null ? '—' : b.narrowMedian.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
              <td>{b.wideActors} · {b.wideMedian == null ? '—' : b.wideMedian.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
              <td class="tot">{배(b.times)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      An actor with more titles draws more lookups whatever those titles did, so the {data.overall.times}×
      in the top row is partly just a count of work. Inside a band the ratios fall to
      {' '}{배(data.bands[1].times)} and {배(data.bands[2].times)} — except the last row, which stays high.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The objection: is the last row recency?</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      Titles that reach many countries are disproportionately recent, and a recent title lifts lookups
      now. So we split every band again by whether the actor’s most recent charting week falls after
      {' '}{data.recentSince}.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Charting titles</th><th>Last charted since {data.recentSince}</th><th>Before that</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr class={b.band === '5 or more' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td class="tot">{배(b.recent.times)}<span class="fine"> · {b.recent.narrowActors} vs {b.recent.wideActors} actors</span></td>
              <td class="tot">{배(b.older.times)}<span class="fine"> · {b.older.narrowActors} vs {b.older.wideActors} actors</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>The objection takes the biggest number away.</b> Among actors with {다섯.band.toLowerCase()} titles,
      reach is worth {배(다섯.recent.times)} if they charted recently and {배(다섯.older.times)} if they did
      not. That is a statement about when they last worked, not about how far the work travelled.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>What survives both controls is the first row.</b> For actors with one charting title the ratio
      holds at {배(하나.recent.times)} and {배(하나.older.times)} — a single title that travelled roughly
      doubles the lookups of a single title that did not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where we refuse to give a number</h2>
    <p class="note">
      A ratio of two medians means nothing on twelve people, so any cell with fewer than
      {' '}{data.minCell} actors on either side is printed as <i>too thin to say</i> rather than as a
      figure. One cell in the table above even runs the other way; with cells this size that is what
      noise looks like, and we would rather show it than tidy it away.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/actor-reach" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix and Wikimedia</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Views are attention, not liking.</b> A page climbs for good news and bad. We report the
        count and never the reason. Views cover {data.viewDays} days, {data.viewPeriod}.
      </li>
      <li><b>The cast list has a known hole.</b> {data.castCaveat}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Cast is attached to {data.castTitles} of {data.castTitlesTotal} titles.</b> An actor whose
        only credits are on the other titles is absent here entirely.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Direction is not established.</b> A widely travelling title may raise an actor’s profile,
        or a known actor may be cast in titles that travel. Nothing here separates those.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/actors">The most looked-up Korean actors</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/where-it-moved">Where Korean titles gained and lost</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
